            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="3" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house is reasonably air tight, however we did identify a few key areas where excess air leakage (and heat loss) is occurrng. These current air leakage locations include:



** The attic hatch does not seal well ( this can best be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** The rear entry door requires a weather-stripping upgrade



** The bathroom exhaust fans on the main floor (especially the one in the master bathroom) allow air leakage and should be upgraded to new Energy Star rated models.



** The 3 x intentional holes in the mechanical room. The 5" chimney for the furnace is required, as is the 5" combustion air supply pipe (the one up high on the wall behind the hot water tank). There is also a 5" outside fresh air supply pipe connected to the return plenum of the furnace. I mentioned the fresh air supply pipe can have an inline damper installed so that you can manually adjust how much it is opened. If you are having condensation building up on your windows, than open it a bit more. If there is no condensation on your windows, than close it a bit more - perhaps all the way to the fully closed position.



Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 159 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 6.6 GJ.



If you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate. Achieving 20% qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.



Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior main floor walls of your house are 2x6 (R20). As per our discussions, you may consider an exterior wall siding &amp; insulation upgrade, in conjunction with your plans to fix the roof. This would be a great time to add another minimum R10 of exterior wall insulation (behind the new siding).



I have modeled the addition of a minimum R10 (eg. 2" of extruded polystyrene rigid foam board) insulation to the above foundation, exterior main floor walls - including the wall that adjoins the rear screened porch.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R10, you could potentially save $ 232 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose to add a minimum R15 or R20, both your rebate &amp; energy savings will increase.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

Your attic space has at least 9" of settled cellulose insulation, which equals about R28 - R3 &amp; I modeled it at the lower end value of R28. My pictures in the attic show this level of insulation extends as far as I could see &amp; zoom into with my camera lens. There is likely going to be some wet &amp; damaged insulation over by the skylight in the kitchen, however our rebate programs do not cover replacement of damaged insulation - only adding a net new value.



With the very low slope of your roof and the 2 sections of scissor truss ceilings, there really isn't much room up there to add more insulation without compromising the attic ventilation along the eaves so I'm not too sure how much more insulation can really be added.



To help you decide upon this, I modeled the benefits of increasing 100% of the ceiling insulation from R28 up to R51 (about 17 inches total).



Savings: Increasing the attic insulation value from R28 up to R51 could potentially save $ 149 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.2 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 250 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade. If you do not to complete a DHERP eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 125  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls are 2x6@12" with R20 insulation. When you are adding a minimum R10 wall insulation to 100% of the perimeter of the upper main floor walls, you will definitely be carrying this down over the above grade portion (almost 50%) of the basement walls.



Savings: By adding a minimum R10 wall insulation to the above grade portion of the basement walls, you could potentially save $ 170 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.0 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 600 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.</foundation>
                <windows>

There are some older triple pane wood windows in the basement that have no Low E coating, and the upper floor windows are older double pane wood windows that appear to have a Low E coating. I decided to model an upgrade to all of the windows (includes the skylight &amp; excludes the patio door) to ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing all of the older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 102 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates (up to 12 existing windows @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for window upgrades.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.</windows>
                <doors>

The front entry on the main floor is an older wooden door that does not close well - it is also a curved top door that will require a wall opening adjustment to accommodate a new Energy Star rectangular door. I recommend you replace this front door with a new Energy Star steel insulated units with a minimum R value of 5.5, and at least double pane windows (triple is better) with low E coatings and argon gas fill between the panes.



Savings: By replacing this older door with higher efficiency Energy Star rated units (as outlined above), you could potentially save $ 111 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.5 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</doors>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have an older Aero oil hot water tank (estimated energy factor (EF) of 0.54), that you are looking to upgrade. As you upgraded your oil furnace in 2017 and have a new double wall oil tank in the basement, you mentioned that staying with oil for your hot water is preferred.



I modeled the option for you to upgrade to a semi on-demand oil hot water heater that is direct vented out the basement wall (no longer needs to co-vent up the chimney with the furnace). Although I don't recommend any one brand, I did mention that I would provide you with an example - the Toyotomi OM-122DW (EF of 0.67) &amp; you can go see one on display at the Thebacha Campus of Aurora College, where they train the new trades trainees on how to install them.



Savings: Installing an eligible on-demand oil hot water heater could potentially save $ 74 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.3 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 750 rebate through the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem />
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has three toilets and the one in your master bedroom is an older unit that use a lot of water &amp; should be replaced with a lower water consumption models. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.



DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:

      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or

      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available



For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $7,350 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,000 for the main floor wall insulation

     - $   600 for the above grade basement wall insulation

     - $   250 for the ceiling insulation

     - $3,000 for replacing 12 windows with new eligible units

     - $   500 for a 10% air leakage reduction



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than you are not eligible for the DHERP - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the above grade basement wall and the ceiling insulation, however these would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - on-demand oil hot water heater - $750

     - ECM domestic water circulation pump - 50% up to $175

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see section further down.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



VENTILATION



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - A kitchen range hood fan

        - 3 bathroom fans

        - A clothes dryer



While conducting the depressurization test (see info further down), the clothes dryer did not generate very much negative pressure, which makes me wonder if the vent pipe is blocked or has come away from the back of the dryer - you will need to check this. If it is blocked, this could result in a fire.



I would recommend you replace the bathroom fans with a new ENERGY STAR?????? rated unit. You may want to consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood, 3 x bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.4 Pascals (see my comments in the ventilation section above about the clothes dryer). As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace does draw vent up a chimney, however there is a dedicated 5" outside combustion air supply pipe located in the mechanical room.



There is also a 5" outside fresh air supply pipe connected to the return plenum ductwork of the furnace, allowing some outside fresh air to come in each time one of the exhaust fans listed above is operated.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke detectors, however did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Smith



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.26 per kWh

Oil  - $0.90 per litre 

Propane - $0.73

Wood - $262.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.46 per bag, $299.62 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



